{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"18b1bcc01afbff5b15213c38cd3534bebf6dae76c6cf7f79753202dec76b23dd","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_03152cf30264b7a9315a13e1b743295ec0bf02ff925b2326f8d0783b566dca1b","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a novel empirical framework for quantifying the computational similarity between human visual perception and ImageNet-trained DNNs. Its primary originality lies in the specific experimental design that uses controlled psychophysical stimuli to isolate perceptual properties from task difficulty, and in the discovery of distinct DNN layer correlates for different perceptual phenomena (mid-computation for change sensitivity, end-computation for shape and contrast).","paper_id":"BJbD_Pqlg","paper_identity_sha256":"44789f13a4086591a0405f335f3217af557d0888ff304257ba135a287fab15a2","prompt_identity_sha256":"0b4a0ad80e1f5eeaa78ef8595f687587214452f8e5e5dcba930619935e34362c","raw_response_sha256":"9443c20fff65c5215727c0afdb2eb8820feb37bc1fb5654642ce3395b1ec5a33","request_identity_sha256":"7eff3d13a33b32612c8d8eef9b9cf09bd3f5ae6b2deb0f42e6dec298b34d62fe","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"fb047d5c0786a9198df53d8fa251b7acc32c48d4f168f2a5c000c4bbf0941fb0","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-005","SRC-007"],"review_text":"The paper introduces a novel research question: whether the system-level computation of human visual perception has specific correlates in the computational stages of trained DNNs. This is framed as a test of the hypothesis that different learning regimes (biological vs. artificial) converge to similar optimal computations when solving general-purpose visual problems. The paper explicitly positions this as a complementary approach to existing representational similarity analyses, aiming to avoid confounds related to task difficulty and categorization consistency.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-008","SRC-016","SRC-020"],"review_text":"The paper does not introduce a new neural network architecture or a novel training algorithm. Instead, it applies standard, pre-trained DNNs (VGG-19, ResNet-152, etc.) to a new analytical task. The methodological contribution is the application of specific metrics (L1 distance and mutual information) to the internal representations of these existing models to quantify their alignment with psychophysical data. This is an application of existing tools rather than a new algorithmic design.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-011","SRC-017","SRC-021","SRC-025"],"review_text":"The paper provides several novel insights into the internal workings of DNNs. It identifies that perceptual sensitivity to image changes correlates with mid-computation layers, whereas sensitivity to shape and contrast correlates with end-computation layers. It also uncovers an unexpected property where logarithmically spaced contrast inputs become linearly spaced at the end-computation layers. Furthermore, it provides a critical assessment by noting that DNNs do not exhibit strong correlates for certain perceptual effects like 3D shape inference and symmetry, highlighting the limits of the convergence hypothesis.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-007","SRC-014","SRC-015"],"review_text":"The paper proposes a novel experimental setup that uses reduced, simplified, and non-natural stimuli (such as sine gratings and line arrays) to ensure identical inherent task difficulty across compared categories. This protocol is designed to prevent the confounding of categorization consistency with measured similarity, which is a significant methodological improvement over previous representational similarity studies that often used natural images.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-005","SRC-006","SRC-029"],"review_text":"The paper combines existing DNN architectures with psychophysical data and specific information-theoretic metrics. The rationale for this combination is well-articulated: by using a DNN as a 'zero-cost' model of perception, the authors can test the convergence hypothesis in a controlled manner. The combination of the L1 metric with the Local Image Masking Database is presented as a way to quantify the applicability of DNNs for estimating perceptual loss, which is a novel synergy between computer vision and vision science.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-025","SRC-026","SRC-027"],"review_text":"The paper includes a critical assessment of the limitations of feedforward DNNs in modeling human perception. It notes that while feedforward DNNs show similarity to certain Gestalt phenomena, the full extent of contextual influence may require recurrent processing, which is not present in the tested models. It also identifies specific perceptual domains (3D and symmetry) where the DNN-human similarity is weak, providing a nuanced view of the convergence hypothesis rather than a blanket claim of similarity.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"f37ff85e28c4831f20c280e4f5862a5ea82e2cf36639c077cef2ea79f0f19d69","paper_id":"BJbD_Pqlg","paper_ordinal":33,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"9443c20fff65c5215727c0afdb2eb8820feb37bc1fb5654642ce3395b1ec5a33","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BJbD_Pqlg","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"270af0c71ede5f27d982031b6f2b02eda0d5556e1cb48d30b26d0493f7d43ddf","request_identity_sha256":"7eff3d13a33b32612c8d8eef9b9cf09bd3f5ae6b2deb0f42e6dec298b34d62fe","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":1095,"prompt_tokens":19215,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":20310},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"7eff3d13a33b32612c8d8eef9b9cf09bd3f5ae6b2deb0f42e6dec298b34d62fe","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}